# Prompt engineering guide

Prompt changes are code changes: define the behavior, test representative inputs, inspect failures,
and keep prompts, tools, evaluators, and runtime configuration consistent.

## Authoring

Applies to text a model will read: agent, skill, and rule files, system prompts, and messages that
dispatch a subagent. Human-facing docs follow their own conventions.

Write for a capable reader with no context. The model sees only the prompt: not the conversation,
the code, or the reasoning that produced it.

- State the objective, what it serves, and what done looks like. Add what the reader cannot infer:
  audience, quality bar, what was already ruled out, and where the task is hard. Spend words on the
  judgment calls, not on what a capable reader does unprompted.
- Give each instruction its reason and its scope. A bare rule is followed rigidly inside the case it
  was written for and applied to cases it was never meant for, because the reader has to guess the
  boundary. Say what the rule protects, when it applies, and the nearest case where it does not.
- Separate hard constraints from defaults. Hard constraints (safety, data, public interfaces) are
  few, hold in every case, and are labeled as such. Everything else is a default the reader adapts.
  A blanket "apply instructions only where relevant" line does not replace this: it carries no
  information about any rule's scope and invites the reader to waive the hard constraints.
- Define the output: format, length, required fields, and the behavior for empty, invalid, and
  ambiguous inputs. Give tool-using agents an escape hatch and require them to say when they cannot
  proceed, so a missing input produces a report instead of an invention.
- Use calm, positive, testable wording. Capitals, absolutes, and repeated warnings make one rule
  outweigh the rest and spread beyond its scope; when something matters, one sentence of reasoning
  does more.
- A single example gets copied closely. Describe the wanted qualities in prose first; when examples
  are needed, give several that differ in meaningful ways and say they are illustrative.
- Put instructions before short context. Put long documents or data first and the question last.
  Separate instructions, examples, and task data with headings or tags.

## Delegation briefs

A prompt that hands work to a subagent or persona is everything the recipient knows. Include:

- the goal and what it serves, and the user's original wording when the task came from the user;
- what done looks like, so the recipient neither stops early nor expands scope;
- what is already known or ruled out, and what makes the task hard;
- constraints: scope, interfaces that must not change, actions that need confirmation;
- the return shape: conclusion first, verified facts marked apart from inferences.

Put every requirement in the brief itself; a recipient skips requirements that live only in a file
it may not load. Treat the returned work as evidence and verify it in proportion to the stakes.

## Instruction revision

- Read the complete artifact and its direct references before changing it, then reassess hierarchy,
  behavior, and scope as a whole. A patch written against one failure usually contradicts or
  duplicates guidance elsewhere, and files that load together and disagree degrade every run.
- Ask what in the prompt produced the failure, and fix that understanding. Revise, merge, reorder,
  or remove guidance instead of appending a rule for one case.
- Before finalizing, remove duplicates, contradictions, stale examples, and instructions that
  cannot be followed together.

## Agent and tool prompts

- Ask agents to ground factual claims in tool output, keep tool arguments bounded and explicit, and
  report required failures. Separate deciding from acting when a step has meaningful side effects.
- Keep deterministic policy, validation, retries, branching, and parallel control flow in code when
  they need no model judgment. Keep tool descriptions to purpose, inputs, outputs, and boundaries.
- For research and diagnosis prompts, ask for competing hypotheses, confidence updated from
  evidence, and important claims verified across sources.

## Program, don't prompt

When an LLM workflow has multiple stages, tools, schemas, or evaluators, treat it as a program:

- Define structured inputs and outputs as a contract. Use field names that explain meaning, and
  types or schemas for constrained values; keep essential requirements out of unbounded prose.
- Keep stages independently inspectable, testable, and replaceable. Separate context assembly, model
  calls, tool use, and output validation when useful.
- Change inference strategies (direct prediction, reasoning, tool use, retry, ensemble) behind the
  same contract and compare them against the same metric.
- Decompose only when it isolates context, enables reuse or inspection, routes work to suitable
  models, or makes a subtask measurable.
- Evaluate the complete workflow, not only a prompt. Save configuration and evaluation metadata when
  results must be reproduced, compared, or rolled back.

## Evaluation and optimization

- Run the prompt and read the outputs before trusting a score; a prompt's only test is what it
  produces. Reread a brief as a reader with no context would.
- Define a metric and baseline before claiming improvement. Test empty, invalid, ambiguous, and
  representative edge cases.
- Separate optimization examples from a held-out evaluation set. Keep model, tools, runtime
  configuration, metric, and split fixed during comparison.
- Keep the baseline when the result is tied, unmeasured, or only improves the optimization set.
  Accept a candidate only when held-out required cases do not regress.
- Use labeled comparisons, deterministic checks, or a separate judge for task quality. Keep
  structural validity checks at the schema or boundary when possible.
- Use targeted examples while iterating, reserve broad suites for regression checks, and inspect
  traces before adding instructions.
- When an optimizer applies (for example DSPy `COPRO`), bound the search: include the baseline, set
  breadth, iterations, and call budget, and record candidates and scores. Verify the installed API
  first, and do not add a framework only to rewrite a prompt by hand.

